#>wonder_trial_valence_word
?["Is a valence word grammar or vocabulary, and what does it name: a material, a status, or a charge?"]{
 #:depth #!semantics ~#lens(comparison)
 ~#readings: ["modifier: boon, bane, bone, bonk and honk lex as a closed class everywhere (seed today), so boon: as a key and #bane-frame as a fragment degrade", "vocabulary: they lex as words, and a valence vocabulary loaded after the parse ($vocab[valence]) gives them meaning where a reader asks", "material: how a component's material changes (the workbench's pentad)", "status: a lifecycle state (VALENCE_PARTICLES)", "charge: the leading slot a reader names charge"]
 ~#grounded: "closed and open classes: function words are a small set the grammar knows and content words an open set the lexicon holds; a word that crosses between them (grammaticalization) changes what can be said around it"
 ~#interactive: "a story host's genre dial weighs paragraphs by valence words, factshift.center draws the lands as force profiles, and a site reads the two lapsed lands (bonk, honk) as a lifecycle's transitions; a vocabulary loaded after the parse lets each host bring its reading without a grammar change"
 ~#forms: ["boon: \"an offering\"", "&[boon, bane]{table}", "@page#bane-frame", "!boon"]
 ~#pairs: ["bonk: \"a bell-note\" / bank: \"a bell-note\": a cast member or a modifier, by one letter", "&[boon, bane]{table} / &[gain, cost]{table}: two names or two modifiers"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "boon: degrades; &[boon, bane]{table} reads, but each word is an empty expression carrying a modifier, not a name; #bane-frame is an error and a prose fallback", site_reader: "the pentad read three ways: material, status, and a slot named charge (ground report, 2026-11-07)", story_reader: "a genre dial weighs boon, bone and bane per paragraph", factshift: "the lands are force profiles that all repel and differ in damping", agent: "_" }
 ~#home: ~"../plan-stream-parse-health/wip.spw"
 ~#judgment: _
 ~#answer: _
 $%[modifier, vocabulary, material, status, charge]
}

#>wonder_trial_dot
?["Is a dot one name with a seam, or two nodes bonded?"]{
 #:depth #!semantics ~#lens(comparison)
 ~#readings: ["one name: seed.water is a single path, and the dot keeps it whole", "bond: seed and water are two nodes the dot ties", "ground: the dot is the . operator, grounding water in seed"]
 ~#grounded: "compounds and possessives: a compound is one word with an internal seam, while a possessive (the seed's water) relates two; readers tell them apart by stress and by what the phrase can do"
 ~#interactive: "a canvas draws one node or two, a wrap breaks at the dot or never, and a drag moves seed.water whole or water alone"
 ~#forms: ["garden{seed.water}", "garden{seed; water}", "garden{.water}", "garden{.water, ~soup}", "garden{ .seed.water }"]
 ~#pairs: ["garden{seed.water} / garden{seed; water}: one part or two", "garden{.water} / garden{.water, ~soup}: the same part alone and in company"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "the parser reads seed.water as one qualified identifier with segments seed and water; the line reader reads garden{seed.water} as two parts joined by a dot, and drops the mark of garden{.water} (journal 2026-11-09-reader-agreement)", site_reader: "the forms page says one name, the dot keeps seed.water whole, and spw.quest wraps the line at the dot; 241 of the 381 bodies in its expression lines are joined by a dot", factshift: "draws three nodes: garden, seed, water", agent: "_" }
 ~#home: ~"../brace-slot-reading/wip.spw"
 ~#judgment: _
 ~#answer: _
 $%[one_name, bond, ground]
}

#>wonder_trial_intermediate_forms
?["Can a line's intermediate forms train attention and invite the next line: is Spw an instrument for thinking as well as a notation?"]{
 #:depth #!pragmatics ~#lens(projection)
 ~#asked: "the owner, 2026-11-08: make the intermediate forms more valuable, with examples or component physics that encourage generative prompting and query writing in Spw's own constructs; Spw's value as a philosophical instrument, building the muscles of creativity and focused attention"
 ~#readings: ["inspection: tokens, slots, features and trace check a reading, and a person looks at them only when something is wrong", "exemplar: each intermediate form is itself a line a person or a model can edit into a query or a prompt (features written as a line, a query as a line, holes as a brief)", "physics: a component's pulls, pushes and heat make a slot's openness felt, so an open slot invites a completion and a settled one rests", "discipline: naming a slot is an act of focused attention, and reading two lines that differ in one slot trains noticing"]
 ~#grounded: "worked and faded examples (an intermediate step shown, then withdrawn as the learner takes it over); contrasting cases prepare people to notice what an explanation later names; divergent then convergent thinking (options held open, then one taken); attention as selection, which the site already counts as holds"
 ~#interactive: "a host shows a line's slot reading beside it and lets a reader drag a slot into a query line or a prompt pack; a held question's options circle until one is taken and the rest stay in view; a newcomer's fuzzy line draws exemplars from mounted scripts"
 ~#forms: ["garden[care]{seed; water}", "?garden[*]{seed}", "?launch[spring]{site; video; event}", "~?[transistors work.how]"]
 ~#pairs: ["garden[care]{seed; water} / its features written as a line: what the middle form lets a reader ask next", "?launch[spring]{site; video; event} held open / with one taken: the divergent and convergent phases of one question"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "source products at three depths (tokens, structure, trace); line.features/1, slot_query and emit holes --brief are planned", site_reader: "a hold is a countable unit of attention, 420 to 560 ms with a destination, and /forms/ is a set of contrasting cases", factshift: "an open question's options circle on the canvas until one is taken", agent: "_" }
 ~#home: ~"../line-kit/wip.spw"
 ~#judgment: _
 ~#answer: _
 $%[inspection, exemplar, physics, discipline]
}

#>wonder_trial_register
?["What does a register say: a frame's shape, the slot it lands in, when it is read, how it was built, where it is written, or who it is for?"]{
 #:depth #!pragmatics ~#lens(comparison)
 ~#asked: "the owner, 2026-11-08: registers could be made more useful"
 ~#readings: ["shape: facet, set, list, map, which the brackets already say, so a stamp names only an exception", "slot: the operator register a frame lands in, by the register bank's access modes (deferred, conditional, resolved, material, ratio)", "phase: when a frame is read (defer, hydrate, pipeline), a step on the lex-to-pragmatic ladder", "construction: how a normal-form node was built (construction, proj, hydrate), so an intermediate form keeps its source shape", "sink: the serializer a frame is written through, as the editor's hover reads it", "voice: who the frame is written for and how it should sound (brief, canon, agent, prose), as register means in linguistics"]
 ~#grounded: "register in linguistics: the variety a speaker chooses for a setting and an audience (formal, technical, intimate); one proposition said in several registers is the same claim for different listeners"
 ~#interactive: "a host renders a frame by its register (a brief for a patron, a card on the site, a field on the canvas); a surface stamped prose tells lint its fallback is meant; a measured register refuses a hand edit; a normal-form register lets an editor show which construction a node came from"
 ~#forms: [".{ carried: 6 }[reg=measured]", "~<rationale>{ prose }[reg=prose]", "^offer{ piece: \"folio\" }[reg=brief]", "#[a, b][reg=list]", "x / y as /(x, y)[reg=proj]"]
 ~#pairs: [".{ carried: 6 }[reg=measured] / %carried[6]: authority by register or by operator", "^offer{…}[reg=brief] / ^offer{…}[reg=canon]: one frame for a patron and for the canon", "}[reg=facet] on .{ } / }[reg=list] on .{ }: a stamp that restates and one that informs"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "normal form stamps nodes [reg=construction], [reg=proj] and [reg=hydrate] (onf.ts); source stamps parse as a trailing frame; 527 workbench stamps say facet and five say defer, hole, proj, pipeline or hydrate; register-bank.spw reads as prose from its .register.bank { line", editor: "the hover calls a stamp a facet qualifier that names a serialization sink (display.ts)", site_reader: "3,814 }[reg=facet] and 2,905 ][reg=set] among 6,890 stamps", factshift: "_", agent: "_" }
 ~#home: ~"../syntax-hardware-alignment/wip.spw"
 ~#judgment: _
 ~#answer: _
 $%[shape, slot, phase, construction, sink, voice]
}

#>wonder_trial_rest_state
?["What is a line's rest state, and who returns it there: the formatter, the writer, or the host?"]{
 #:depth #!pragmatics ~#lens(comparison)
 ~#readings: ["formatted: the canonical form spw format writes is the rest state, and a reset is a format that moves no reading", "authored: the bytes the writer left are the rest state, only the writer resets, and tools keep every byte (write-back identity)", "host: each host keeps its own rest (a canvas settles when its heat runs out, a page reads with scripts off), and the reset is the host's", "none: a line is never at rest, and every reading is a visit"]
 ~#grounded: "a museum floor's reset: stewards return parts to their places between visitors, so the next one starts fresh without undoing what the exhibit is (docs/theory/spw/exhibit-floor.spw, steward)"
 ~#interactive: "a host offers a steward view after a session (what moved, what reads as prose, what was touched most) and a reset that moves no reading"
 ~#forms: ["garden{ seed ;water }", "garden{seed; water}"]
 ~#pairs: ["garden{ seed ;water } / garden{seed; water}: one line, untidy and at rest"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "spw format writes a canonical form; line-kit keeps the writer's bytes on write-back; reading-drift plans a fingerprint that formatting must not move", site_reader: "every room reads with scripts off", factshift: "the canvas settles when its heat runs out, and the picture holds", agent: "_" }
 ~#home: ~"../line-kit/wip.spw"
 ~#judgment: _
 ~#answer: _
 $%[formatted, authored, host, none]
}

#>wonder_trial_play_schemas
?["Do Spw's marks mirror the schemas children repeat in play: trajectory, transporting, enclosing, connecting, positioning, transforming?"]{
 #:depth #!pragmatics ~#lens(projection)
 ~#readings: ["mirror: each mark family follows a schema (connectors trajectory, carried lines transporting, containers enclosing, joins connecting, slot order positioning, operators transforming), so newcomers reach for marks in schema order", "gloss: the analogy makes the marks memorable but predicts nothing", "curriculum: the schemas order a sequence of marks to teach, whether or not the marks mirror them"]
 ~#grounded: "play schemas in early childhood (Athey, building on Piaget): repeated patterns of action through which children build concepts, seen wherever children carry, line up, wrap and send things (docs/theory/spw/exhibit-floor.spw, play_schemas)"
 ~#interactive: "a quest introduces marks in schema order, and a canvas answers each schema with its own physics: a trajectory slides, an enclosure holds, a transport carries"
 ~#forms: ["a -> b", "home{seed}", "[mode]home", "a ~ b", "!map{stew}"]
 ~#pairs: ["home{seed} / home seed: enclosed or loose", "a -> b / a b: a path or two things", "[mode]home / home[mode]: positioned before or after"]
 ~#readers_today: .{ seed: "each form reads; no tool orders marks for a newcomer", site_reader: "_", factshift: "each mark tilts the field, and joins draw as ties", agent: "_" }
